1
00:00:01,880 --> 00:00:03,260
Your laptop opens a file

2
00:00:03,280 --> 00:00:05,700
of a million orders in a second.

3
00:00:05,720 --> 00:00:07,540
Now open every order your company

4
00:00:07,560 --> 00:00:10,180
has ever taken: two terabytes.

5
00:00:10,200 --> 00:00:11,500
It doesn't fit in memory,

6
00:00:11,520 --> 00:00:13,860
and one machine would take all night.

7
00:00:13,880 --> 00:00:16,020
So a lot of teams split the work across

8
00:00:16,040 --> 00:00:18,840
many machines, with Apache Spark.

9
00:00:19,420 --> 00:00:20,800
Hi, I'm Chris.

10
00:00:20,820 --> 00:00:22,200
I've spent over 10 years

11
00:00:22,220 --> 00:00:23,160
in data engineering

12
00:00:23,180 --> 00:00:26,040
and interviewed more than 200 engineers.

13
00:00:26,060 --> 00:00:27,800
Now I run Stackle, where I teach

14
00:00:27,820 --> 00:00:30,300
the skills that actually get you hired.

15
00:00:30,757 --> 00:00:31,897
Spark is an open-source

16
00:00:31,917 --> 00:00:33,017
engine for processing

17
00:00:33,037 --> 00:00:34,137
large amounts of data

18
00:00:34,157 --> 00:00:35,657
across many machines.

19
00:00:35,677 --> 00:00:37,577
You write Python or SQL,

20
00:00:37,597 --> 00:00:40,457
and Scala, Java and R work too.

21
00:00:40,477 --> 00:00:43,137
It started at UC Berkeley in 2009,

22
00:00:43,157 --> 00:00:44,897
was open-sourced in 2010,

23
00:00:44,917 --> 00:00:46,097
and became a top-level

24
00:00:46,117 --> 00:00:48,257
Apache project in 2014.

25
00:00:48,277 --> 00:00:49,817
Here's the core idea.

26
00:00:49,837 --> 00:00:52,137
Spark cuts your data into partitions.

27
00:00:52,157 --> 00:00:53,937
You write steps on a DataFrame,

28
00:00:53,957 --> 00:00:55,257
which works like a table:

29
00:00:55,277 --> 00:00:57,337
filter, group, sum.

30
00:00:57,357 --> 00:00:59,577
But Spark doesn't run them one by one.

31
00:00:59,597 --> 00:01:00,697
It writes a plan,

32
00:01:00,717 --> 00:01:01,977
and runs it only when you

33
00:01:01,997 --> 00:01:04,537
ask for a result, like saving the output.

34
00:01:04,557 --> 00:01:06,777
Then one task per partition runs,

35
00:01:06,797 --> 00:01:08,657
all at the same time.

36
00:01:08,677 --> 00:01:10,164
Now, the architecture.

37
00:01:10,184 --> 00:01:11,684
The driver runs your code,

38
00:01:11,704 --> 00:01:13,657
and turns the plan into tasks.

39
00:01:13,677 --> 00:01:14,697
The cluster manager,

40
00:01:14,717 --> 00:01:16,377
like Kubernetes or YARN,

41
00:01:16,397 --> 00:01:17,897
gives the driver executors

42
00:01:17,917 --> 00:01:19,257
on worker machines.

43
00:01:19,277 --> 00:01:20,804
Executors run the tasks,

44
00:01:20,824 --> 00:01:23,257
and keep data in memory, so it's fast.

45
00:01:23,277 --> 00:01:25,310
And if one executor dies?

46
00:01:25,330 --> 00:01:26,777
The driver re-runs just

47
00:01:26,797 --> 00:01:28,217
its tasks on another one,

48
00:01:28,237 --> 00:01:30,457
rebuilding the lost pieces from the plan.

49
00:01:30,477 --> 00:01:32,697
Then the results land in storage.

50
00:01:32,717 --> 00:01:35,177
So when a team says, "we use Spark,"

51
00:01:35,197 --> 00:01:36,697
it means their data is too

52
00:01:36,717 --> 00:01:37,977
big for one machine.

53
00:01:37,997 --> 00:01:39,764
They write the steps once.

54
00:01:39,784 --> 00:01:40,897
Spark splits them into

55
00:01:40,917 --> 00:01:42,324
tasks across a cluster,

56
00:01:42,344 --> 00:01:44,617
and re-runs any piece that fails.

57
00:01:44,637 --> 00:01:46,477
That's Apache Spark.

58
00:01:48,537 --> 00:01:50,677
So, what should I explain next?

59
00:01:50,697 --> 00:01:51,837
Tell me in the comments below,

60
00:01:51,857 --> 00:01:54,057
and it could be the next episode.
